{"name":"Not so Innocent","hex":"#6a6968","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"not","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Nomatsiguenga."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["Negates the meaning of the modified verb.","To no degree."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/not"},{"term":"innocent","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Free from guilt, sin, or immorality.","Bearing no legal responsibility for a wrongful act."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One who is innocent, especially a young child.","A harmless simple-minded person; an idiot."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/innocent"}],"entries":[{"title":"Innocence","blocks":[{"text":"Innocence is a lack of guilt, with respect to any kind of crime, or wrongdoing. In a legal context, innocence is prior to the sense of legal guilt and is a primal emotion connected with the sense of self. It is often confused as being the opposite of the guilt of an individual, with respect to a crime. In other contexts, it is a lack of experience."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Prud%27hon%2C_Pierre_Paul_-_Venus_Bathing_or_Innocence_-_c._1810.JPG/330px-Prud%27hon%2C_Pierre_Paul_-_Venus_Bathing_or_Innocence_-_c._1810.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Innocence"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Bouguereau-Linnocence.jpg/500px-Bouguereau-Linnocence.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Bouguereau-Linnocence.jpg/500px-Bouguereau-Linnocence.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Bouguereau's L'Innocence: Women, young children, and lambs are all symbols of innocence.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABouguereau-Linnocence.jpg